From: Remora <noreply@localhost>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 08:55:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] VideoFramePool: survive a graphic block with no pool data

getBufferIdFromGraphicBlock()'s GRALLOC/SECURE_LINEAR arm called
_C2BlockFactory::GetGraphicBlockPoolData(block) and dereferenced the result
immediately, with no null check -- while the very next statement carefully
null-checks the BufferPoolData it fetches. On the Remora bare/NVIDIA path the
decoder's blocks come back with no pool data at all, so that dereference took
down the whole c2-va HAL: SIGSEGV in VideoFramePool::getBufferIdFromGraphicBlock
+93, thread VideoFramePoolF, every time an app fed it video. NIKKE's startup
movie killed the service twice inside 35 s, and each death/respawn tore down the
video surface -- what the user saw as a heavy black flicker (bd remora-3fe6).

Returning nullopt alone would stop the crash but abandon the decode request, so
hardware video would silently never work. Instead identify the buffer the way
the BUFFERQUEUE arm directly above already does: by its dma-buf. The id only has
to be stable and unique per buffer for the mBuffers / shouldDropBuffer
bookkeeping, and the dma-buf inode is exactly that -- the same identity the
in-image composer keys on. The handle is null-checked too, so the fallback
cannot reintroduce the fault it replaces.

Verified on A17 bare/NVIDIA: before, two HAL tombstones per NIKKE launch; after,
the service holds one pid across the whole run, zero new tombstones, and
c2.remora.vaapi.avc.decoder stays the decoder in use (no software fallback).
---
diff --git a/components/VideoFramePool.cpp b/components/VideoFramePool.cpp
index 8778118..fe616cb 100644
--- a/components/VideoFramePool.cpp
+++ b/components/VideoFramePool.cpp
@@ -48,6 +48,21 @@ std::optional<uint32_t> VideoFramePool::getBufferIdFromGraphicBlock(C2BlockPool&
     case V4L2AllocatorId::SECURE_LINEAR: {
         std::shared_ptr<_C2BlockPoolData> blockPoolData =
                 _C2BlockFactory::GetGraphicBlockPoolData(block);
+        if (!blockPoolData) {
+            // A gralloc-allocated block carries no pool data, so identify it the way the
+            // BUFFERQUEUE arm above does: by its dma-buf. The id only has to be stable and
+            // unique per buffer for mBuffers/shouldDropBuffer bookkeeping, and the dma-buf
+            // inode is exactly that. Without this the null deref killed the whole HAL.
+            if (!block.handle()) {
+                ALOGE("Obtained C2GraphicBlock has neither pool data nor a handle.");
+                return std::nullopt;
+            }
+            auto dmabufId = android::getDmabufId(block.handle()->data[0]);
+            if (!dmabufId) {
+                return std::nullopt;
+            }
+            return dmabufId.value();
+        }
         if (blockPoolData->getType() != _C2BlockPoolData::TYPE_BUFFERPOOL) {
             ALOGE("Obtained C2GraphicBlock is not bufferpool-backed.");
             return std::nullopt;
-- 
